POST-TABLE-1 ADDENDUM, appended after t1 ran.  The block above is unchanged; this is
the measurement, including where the block's own predictions were WRONG.

*** TABLE 1 FOUND A FLOOR BREACH: M_ICSR ESCAPED 47/47, AND THIS BLOCK PREDICTED 43. ***

  BASE      47/47
  M_OLD     14/47   all 32 kill rows + `msg silent'
  M_HALF    30/47   the four re-admitted offsets x 2 directions x 2 rows, + `msg silent'
  M_ICSR    47/47   *** TOTAL ESCAPE -- clause (d) floor breach, L2 ***
  M_NULL    22/47   live, nvram0, four devsig, the 1fef/1ff9 accept pair, 16 read rows
  HEAD      14/47   whole-file null medium
  RESTORED  47/47   restore verified, sentinel cleared on both parts

TWO PREDICTIONS TO SCORE HONESTLY:
  * M_HALF was predicted at "39/47 ... no, 30/47", written with the uncertainty stated
    rather than resolved.  MEASURED 30/47.  The arithmetic that got there was right and
    the first number was not; recording both is the point of writing predictions down.
  * M_ICSR was predicted 43/47 on the reasoning that the `ok rd/wr 1ff8' pair would
    redden.  **MEASURED 47/47.  The prediction was WRONG and the reason is instructive.**

WHY M_ICSR ESCAPED, derived rather than guessed:

    FIXED:   accept if  addr < 0x1ff8 || addr >= 0x1ff9   -> switch sees ONLY 0x1ff8
    M_ICSR:  accept if  addr < 0x1ff8 || addr >= 0x1ff8   -> A TAUTOLOGY

  Every address is accepted, the switch is NEVER REACHED, and 0x1ff8 is served as plain
  RAM.  The control register silently stops doing its work.

  *** AND THE ROWS AIMED AT THAT OFFSET COULD NOT SEE IT, BY CONSTRUCTION. ***  `ok rd
  1ff8' and `ok wr 1ff8' assert only that the access RAN TO COMPLETION -- which is true on
  the RAM path too.  They are SURVIVE-ONLY at that offset, which is this project's own
  named vacuity: a survival oracle cannot separate two arms that both survive.  The
  CHANNEL block above named their channel as "the implemented case stops being reached;
  exit/obs change", and that was simply false -- neither exit status nor the scratch
  observable moves.  **A channel asserted at writing time and never checked is exactly
  what the CHANNEL rule exists to catch, and here the rule was applied and got the answer
  wrong.**  What would have caught it is asking not "can this row vary?" but "can it vary
  UNDER THIS MUTANT?" -- the mutant-specific form.

THE REPAIR, in-round under L2: ONE ROW, `csr rise', reading the ICSR case's SIDE EFFECT.
Writing the READ bit 0 -> 1 calls mk48txx_update_regs(), refreshing the clock registers
from the host.  So the row clears ICSR, POISONS a clock slot, drives the rising edge, and
reads the slot back:

    fixed build   -> the poison is gone, replaced by the host value   (0x10 today)
    M_ICSR        -> the poison SURVIVES, because no update ever ran  (0x5a)

  It needs no new instruction encoding: r5's low byte is 0x5a, and 0x5a & MK48TXX_CSR_READ
  (0x40) is 0x40, so one ST_B of r5 both poisons a slot and drives the edge.  0x5a is a
  safe poison because a BCD day-of-month never exceeds 0x31.

  MEASURED GREEN on the fixed build before table 2: the detector is 48/48.

TABLE 2 RE-RUNS EVERY LEG, not just M_ICSR, because this project has measured that added
rows perturb other mutants' scores.  Table 1 is preserved at t1/ rather than overwritten.

