  BASE      48/48
  M_OLD     15/48   32 kill rows + `msg silent'
  M_HALF    31/48   the four re-admitted offsets, both directions, both rows + `msg silent'
  M_ICSR    47/48   *** KILLED, by `csr rise' AND BY NOTHING ELSE ***
  M_NULL    22/48   live, nvram0, four devsig, the accept pair, `csr rise', 16 read rows,
                    `msg silent'
  HEAD      15/48   whole-file null medium
  RESTORED  48/48   cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, sentinel cleared on both parts

*** THE NEW ROW IS NECESSARY AND NON-REDUNDANT, which is the thing worth checking about
any row added to close a breach. ***  M_ICSR scores 47/48 with `csr rise' as its ONLY red:
no other row in the instrument sees that mutant, so the row is not decoration and the
breach was real.  Conversely `csr rise' is green under M_OLD, M_HALF and HEAD -- those
leave 0x1ff8 reaching the switch -- so it is aimed rather than indiscriminate.

AND THE ADDED ROW DID NOT PERTURB THE OTHER LEGS' KILL SETS.  Every other score rose by
EXACTLY ONE (14->15, 30->31, 47->48) and every FAILING list is unchanged except for
M_NULL, which gains `csr rise' because removing the backing store also removes the poison
write the row depends on.  That is the expected coupling and it is stated rather than
discovered later.  *This project has measured that added rows change other mutants'
scores; here the change is exactly the new row's own denominator, and the re-table is what
establishes that rather than assuming it.*

THE FLOOR, now satisfied and each clause pointed at its leg:
